    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Gahanna was founded in 1849 along Big Walnut Creek by John Clark, who purchased 800 acres from Governor Worthington in 1814.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2,
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gahanna%2C_Ohio",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The city’s name derives from a Native American word meaning “three creeks joining into one,” a theme echoed in its seal that reads “Three In One.”.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11,
        12,
        22
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gahanna%2C_Ohio",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1881, 55 citizens petitioned Franklin County to incorporate Gahanna, and the village was officially recorded on August 8, 1881.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        26,
        27
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gahanna%2C_Ohio",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "In 2022, Gahanna received its first Ohio Historical Marker, honoring the Big Walnut Country Club and its founders.",
